Chapter: PrismHosted
  cites Prism chapter PrismRoutes
  cites Works chapter HostedServe

 The HOSTED entry. Prism served from a static Linux ELF or a Windows console
 .exe, over the host's TCP, with no NIC and no bare-metal boot beneath it.

 THE ROUTE FUNCTION IS THE SAME ONE `Prism` SERVES. Both entries call
 `prism-route sources ir-cache`, and neither the catalogue nor the routes name
 a target. That is the property the split exists to hold: `Prism` cites
 `Net chapter WebServer` and calls `web-serve`, this chapter cites
 `Works chapter HostedServe` and calls `hosted-serve`, and nothing else
 differs between them.

 The port derives from `hosted-kind`, 1 on Linux and 2 on Windows, because WSL2
 forwards a listener inside WSL onto the Windows host and holds the host's port
 after the subject exits. The account is in `codex/test/hosted-echo.codex`.

 The bare-metal entry serves on 9200 through a host-side proxy. A hosted binary
 needs no proxy, because a hosted server IS the host's socket, so the port is
 the one a browser reaches directly.

 We say:

Section: Entry

  prism-hosted-port-base : Integer = 9200

  opening : [Console, Network.Write] Integer = act
    let sources = prism-make-path-entries prism-source-paths 0 []
    in let ir-cache = prism-make-path-entries prism-ir-paths 0 []
    in let port = prism-hosted-port-base + hosted-kind
    in act
      print-line-uni ("[prism] " & show (list-length sources) & " source paths catalogued")
      fd <- hosted-listen port
      print-line-uni (if fd >= 0 then "listen 0" else "listen " & integer-to-text fd)
      served <- hosted-serve-on (prism-route sources ir-cache) fd (0 - 1)
      served
    end
  end

